                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Now, first of all, since this is not mentioned anywhere on the store page, the actual name (from the game credits) is The Girl and the Robot - Act 1. Game ends on a \"To be continued\" and the developer says on the forums that the Act 2 will be most likely a separate game. This seemingly intentional \"shyness\" to say what the game is, however, is not why I cannot recommend it.\n\nI cannot recommend it because it doesn't feel like a proper made for release game to be sold for actual money.\n\nThe Girl and the Robot feels like a suprisingly long (3 hours) game jam game, made by inexperienced but talanted people as a \"hey, a cool game can be made out of this\" demo. Except no cool game was made out of this, this IS the game. And it's a frustrating experience from start to non-existent finish.\n\nControls and animations never feel right. Combat is either easy (because it's dumb and seemingly made to be exploited) or stupid annoying and based on luck (because the encounter is designed to remove player-favorable exploits but leave in enemy-favorable ones). Occasional jumping moments feel random with it being as easy to overshoot the target as undershoot, depending on if you try using the rather badly done running before or not. \"Puzzles\" are more tedious, then challenging, with one requiring you to constantly switch between characters to see where to go, watching slow transitional animations every time. And The Girl simply dies if any enemy touches her once. Now way to escape, no way to save her even if when standing one step away as The Robot. She's touched - Game Over. Restart from a usually stupidly far away checkpoint.\n\nThe roughness of the game is obvious even in how every single transition in every single cutscene is a sudden gray screen for few seconds and how several locations lag horribly due to optimization issues. And in how so many things are just \"there\", how many things make no sound at all. How the world, despite the pretty art style, simply feels like a fast made map you'd find in a mod or a old multiplayer map, not in a puzzle and exploratory singleplayer game that really wants to look good.\n\nThe Girl and the Robot is not fun to play. It's not finished. It doesn't even feel like a game, rather than a concept, something to show the investors to get money for the actual game. It doesn't achieve what it promises, doesn't come even close. And it's a damn shame.",
